Register with `.on(name, fn)`, fire with `.emit(name, ...args)` — every argument after the event name is passed to the listeners:
```js
myEmitter.on('event', (a, b) => console.log(a, b));
myEmitter.emit('event', 'a', 'b');
```

question:
What happens here?
```js
const myEmitter = new EventEmitter();
myEmitter.emit('error', new Error('whoops!'));
```

explanation:
`'error'` is the one special event name: emitting it with no `'error'` listener registered throws the error, and an uncaught throw takes down the process. That's why robust code always attaches one:
```js
myEmitter.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error('whoops! there was an error');
});
```
This bites in practice with streams and sockets, which emit `'error'` on their own.

```js
myEmitter.once('event', () => console.log('only once'));
myEmitter.emit('event'); // logs
myEmitter.emit('event'); // ignored

const cb = () => console.log('connected!');
server.on('connection', cb);
server.off('connection', cb);
```
The need for the same function reference is why anonymous inline listeners can't be removed individually — name them if you'll need to detach.

```js
myEmitter.on('event', function () {
  console.log(this === myEmitter); // true
});
myEmitter.on('event', () => {
  console.log(this); // outer scope's this
});
```
Use a regular function when you want emitter methods on `this`; use an arrow when you want your class instance's `this` from the surrounding method.

```js
import { once, EventEmitter } from 'node:events';

const ee = new EventEmitter();
process.nextTick(() => ee.emit('myevent', 42));
const [value] = await once(ee, 'myevent'); // 42
```
It resolves with the array of emit arguments and supports cancellation via `AbortSignal` (`once(ee, 'foo', { signal })` rejects with `AbortError` on abort). A clean bridge from event-style APIs into async/await code.

```js
readFile('/tmp/hello.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err; // or handle gracefully
  console.log(data);
});
```
The convention is what made Node's callback world composable — utilities like `util.promisify` rely on it to convert callback APIs into promise APIs mechanically.

